Allow O_PATH traversal handles on directories leading to read roots.

When `denyRead` names paths below `/` (e.g. `$HOME`, `/run/user/<uid>/...`),
`LoweredReadAccess::restricted` drops `/` from the read roots and splits it
into the siblings of each denied path. The directories on the way to those
denials (`/`, `/home`, `/run`, `/run/user/<uid>`, ...) end up under no read
root, so the seccomp broker refuses every `openat` of them as `allow_miss`,
including `O_PATH | O_DIRECTORY`, which reads nothing.

Nix 2.35 resolves every path beneath a root descriptor opened on `/`
(`makeFSSourceAccessor("/")`, then `openat2(..., RESOLVE_BENEATH)`). It
already falls back to an `O_PATH | O_DIRECTORY` "traversable" handle when the
`O_RDONLY` open fails with EACCES, but the broker refuses that too, so every
evaluation that touches the filesystem dies with `opening file "/":
Permission denied`.

Let `O_PATH | O_DIRECTORY` opens through when the target is a strict ancestor
of a read root and is not read-denied. Such a handle only anchors `*at` calls,
which the broker mediates on their own. `O_DIRECTORY` keeps it off regular
files, which could otherwise be hardlinked elsewhere and read. Listing those
directories (`O_RDONLY`) and hard `denyReadAlways` matches stay denied.

Candidate for upstream as a bug fix. See package/overlay/landstrip/overlay.nix.

